<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-proteins-and-carbohydrates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Carbohydrates and Proteins</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous section, we reintroduced water and lipids.
      Let's close off the major macromolecules by discussing carbohydrates and proteins.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-proteins-in-hair-and-food-are-different">
    <title>The Proteins in Hair and Food Are Different</title>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="subsec-hair-primarily-consists-of-keratin-fibers"/>, we briefly introduced proteins as chains of amino acids connected by peptide bonds.
      Keratin is a protein that exists in the hair, giving it its physical properties.
      <idx><h>protein</h></idx> <idx><h>keratin</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall that keratin forms tight superhelical rope-like structures that make hair incredibly strong.
      Yet, other proteins exist <mdash/> such as those in eggs and meat <mdash/> why then, do the proteins in meat not behave the same way as those in keratin?
    </p>

    <p>
      The answer is that due to the specific types of amino acids in the protein, the structures are different.
      Most food proteins, such as <term>ovalbumin</term> (in eggs) or <term>myoglobin</term> (in meat), form 3D origami bundles instead of rigid superhelices.
      These proteins are called <term>globular proteins</term>.
      <idx><h>ovalbumin</h></idx> <idx><h>myoglobin</h></idx> <idx><h>protein</h><h>globular</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Just like in hair, the structures are created by the same forces <mdash/> weak hydrogen bonds, ionic salt bridges, and covalent disulfide bridges.
      These interactions may rely on specific atoms in different amino acids (disulfide bridges, for example, form between the <m>\ce{-SH}</m> atoms between two cysteine residues, as we discussed in <xref ref="subsec-disulfide-bridges-account-for-permanent-hair-curl"/>).
      As different amino acids have slightly different atomic configurations, their interactions change in a way to modify how the protein is structured as a whole.
    </p>

    <p>
      The details of protein structure are a bit complicated, but you can read more about it in <xref ref="subsec-protein-structure-is-hierarchical"/>.
      As a result of these forces, proteins are some of the most structurally and functionally diverse molecules in biology.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
